<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lachlan Macquarie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lachlan Macquarie was a Scottish army officer who served as Governor of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> from 1810 to 1821, after the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-bligh">William Bligh</a>. He restored order, built roads, churches and public buildings and sponsored the first crossing of the Blue Mountains. He favoured the readmission of freed convicts to society, which brought him into conflict with free settlers and the British government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lachlan-macquarie.jpg" alt="Lachlan Macquarie" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Opie? · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ln-Governor-Lachlan_macquarie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lachlan Macquarie was born on 31 January 1762 on the island of Ulva off the coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-mull">Isle of Mull</a>. His father was a carpenter and miller and his mother Margaret the sister of Murdoch Maclaine, laird of Lochbuie. He joined the British Army in 1776 and served in the American War of Independence, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1801, where he was Deputy Adjutant-General of a British-Indian force sent against the French. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1803 with a fortune of £20,000, and in 1807 travelled overland from India through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Appointed Governor of New South Wales in 1809, Macquarie arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-cove">Sydney Cove</a> on 28 December with his own regiment, the 73rd. The government had chosen an army officer to win over the New South Wales Corps, whose officers had mutinied against and imprisoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-bligh">William Bligh</a> in the Rum Rebellion. He planned the street layout of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>, built Macquarie Street, and created Hyde Park as the first public park. He promoted marriage and church attendance, and appointed emancipists to office: Francis Greenway became colonial architect and William Redfern colonial surgeon.</p><p>In 1813 he authorised the crossing of the Blue Mountains by Gregory Blaxland, William Wentworth and William Lawson, and in 1815 he ordered the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjul">Bathurst</a>, the first inland British settlement. He called for Aboriginal people to be treated with kindness, yet his orders led to the Appin Massacre of Gundungurra and Dharawal people. His championing of emancipists and heavy public spending aroused opposition in the colony and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and in 1819 Earl Bathurst sent John Bigge to inquire into the colony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Macquarie returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1824 while defending himself against Bigge's charges. His reputation grew after his death, especially among emancipists and their descendants. Many regard him as the most enlightened of the early governors, and he formally adopted the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> for the continent, first using it in an official despatch in 1817. Many places and institutions, among them Macquarie University, Lake Macquarie and the Macquarie River, are named after him. A 2012 statue in Sydney's Hyde Park has drawn criticism over his punitive expeditions against Indigenous people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lachlan Macquarie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Lachlan Macquarie</b> built roads and public buildings and promoted freed convicts.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-new-south-wales">Colony of New South Wales</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ulva, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.48,-6.21&amp;year=1802" aria-label="Ulva on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4416 244 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lachlan Macquarie"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4866 514h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4866 514h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.48,-6.21&amp;year=1802">What was here in 1802? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
